186045417] [17592186149616 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186496846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149616 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0e931abd-da31-5b58-811b-525bdcda10eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150521 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186150522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150521 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186150523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150521 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186150524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150521 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186150525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150521 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186150526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150521 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150521 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150521 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186150524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150521 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e778c54c-5e65-5f06-b065-5f88bd929999" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150522 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150522 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150522 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c2f11db9-07b9-5b0d-a5f7-602cade7cf60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150523 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150523 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150523 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c6eb5674-aafb-5690-8e5d-415222b6e28c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150524 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150524 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150524 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d8e565e1-892e-5a47-b36c-468b0d74f4d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150525 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150525 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150525 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0b2a4fb8-1c72-53c3-a951-1cc38d337d51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150526 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150526 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150526 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5b475ba3-706f-5643-bb46-ec68ac316100" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151827 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13125" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151827 #edb/ref 1071 "| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $6$ | $4$ |\n| $9$ | $6$ |\n| $15$ | $10$ |\n\nThe table above shows a proportional relationship. Assuming that $y=kx$ for each pair of values, what is the constant of proportionality?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151827 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5676578d-255c-51e8-a64f-60e273dabb90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151827 #edb/ref 1074 "We're told that $x$ and $y$ have a proportional relationship.\n\nTo find the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ from the table. Let's choose the point $(6,4),$ which corresponds to the first row of the table.\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{4÷2}{6÷2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is $k=\\dfrac{2}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151828 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13126" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151828 #edb/ref 1071 "Martha is running low on cupcakes, so she decides to make some more. The amount of time $h$ (in hours) she spends baking and the number of cupcakes $c$ she makes are shown in the table below.\n\n| Hours $(h)$ | Cupcakes $(c)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $5$ |\n| $3$ | $7$ |\n| $4$ | $9$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. There is a proportional relationship between $c$ and $h.$\n\nII. There is **not** a proportional relationship between $c$ and $h.$\n\nIII. The constant of proportionality is $\\dfrac{9}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151828 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6bbbe009-2488-5442-83e9-98606ca5e78b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151828 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's add a new column to our table and compute the ratio of cupcakes per hour for each row.\n\n| Hours $(h)$ | Cupcakes $(c)$ | Cupcakes / Hours |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $5$ | $\\dfrac 5 2$ |\n| $3$ | $7$ | $\\dfrac 7 3$ |\n| $4$ | $9$ | $\\dfrac 9 4$ |\n\nThe ratio of cupcakes per hour is not the same for each row. Therefore, there is *not* a proportional relationship between $c$ and $h.$ Consequently, there is no constant of proportionality.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151829 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13127" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151829 #edb/ref 1071 "Merlin is making sandwiches for a party. There is a proportional relationship between the time $x$ (in minutes) he spends preparing sandwiches and the number of sandwiches $y$ he makes.\n\n| Time $(x)$ | Sandwiches $(y)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $4$ |\n| $10$ | $8$ |\n| $15$ | $12$ |\n\nWhat is the equation for the relationship between $x$ and $y?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151829 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1c57db95-1fdb-5ea4-894e-0f137b3ef795" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151829 #edb/ref 1074 "We're told that $x$ and $y$ have a proportional relationship.\n\nEvery proportional relationship can be written in the form $y=kx,$ where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.\n\nTo compute the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ from the table. Let's choose the point $(5,4),$ which corresponds to the first row of the table.\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality as follows:\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{y}{x} = \\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation relating $x$ and $y$ is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{4}{5} x.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151830 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13128" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151830 #edb/ref 1071 "Jill likes to listen to music while she runs. The following table shows a proportional relationship between the distance $d$ (in kilometers) she runs and the number of songs $s$ she listens to.\n\n| Kilometers ($d$) | Songs ($s$) |\n| --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ |\n| $4$ | $6$ |\n| $6$ | $9$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The unit rate is  $\\dfrac32$ songs per kilometer.\n\nII. If Jill runs for $1$ kilometer, she'll listen to $1.5$ songs.\n\nIII. If Jill runs for $12$ kilometers, she'll listen to $15$ songs." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151830 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "198d2eae-92a5-57bd-a165-eae20c9a7078" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151830 #edb/ref 1074 "We're told that $d$ and $s$ have a proportional relationship.\n\nTo find the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(d,s)$ from the table. Let's choose the point $(2,3),$ which corresponds to the first row of the table. \n\nWe can find the constant of proportionality as follows:\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{s}{d} = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nThe units of $k$ are \"songs per kilometer\" since we divided the number of songs $s$ by the distance $d.$ Therefore, the relationship between $d$ and $s$ is \n\n$$\ns=\\dfrac32 d.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. We found above that $k =\\dfrac32$ songs per kilometer.\n\n- Statement II is true. We know that $k=\\dfrac32,$ which means that if Jill runs for $1$ kilometer, she'll listen to $\\dfrac32 = 1.5$ songs.\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $d=12$ into the relationship $s=\\dfrac32 d,$ we get \n\n$$\ns=\\dfrac32\\cdot  12=18\n$$\n\nwhich means that she'll listen to $18$ songs if she runs for $12$ kilometers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152142 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152142 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing a Constant of Proportionality" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152142 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152142 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2f15be3c-52df-59c0-82a7-d48736082795" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152143 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152143 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152143 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152143 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining Whether a Table Represents a Proportional Relationship" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152143 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152143 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a19a2e0a-2271-567a-bd59-12ccbac62ba5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152144 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152144 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152144 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152144 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Proportional Relationship Equation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152144 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152144 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "48d2a1f5-e39d-523c-81ed-09426a8045f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152145 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152145 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152145 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152145 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152145 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152145 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152145 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152145 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152145 #edb/ref 1039 "Interpreting Data From Proportional Relationships" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152145 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152145 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bea5c8e3-3a8e-58a6-b289-84137c2b60cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152311 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152311 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152311 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152311 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152311 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152311 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152311 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152311 #edb/ref 1045 "Understanding Proportional Relationships Using Tables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152311 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186153033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152311 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152311 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152311 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e1e5c1d4-ae13-5c28-8065-9304d1a284b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152520 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152520 #edb/ref 1069 "q-235779" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152520 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152520 #edb/ref 1071 "| $u$ | $v$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $6$ | $8$ |\n| $9$ | $12$ |\n| $12$ | $16$ |\n\nThe table above shows a proportional relationship. Assuming that $v=ku$ for each pair of values, what is the constant of proportionality? \n\nThe constant of proportionality is \n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152520 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "27248ef4-661d-5726-b713-0834a355482a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152520 #edb/ref 1074 "We're told that $u$ and $v$ have a proportional relationship.\n\nTo find the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(u,v)$ from the table. Let's choose the point $(6,8),$ which corresponds to the first row of the table.\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $v$-coordinate to the $u$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{v}{u} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{8÷2}{6÷2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is $k=\\frac{4}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152522 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152522 #edb/ref 1069 "q-235938" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152522 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152522 #edb/ref 1071 "Elena is painting her fence. There is a proportional relationship between the volume of paint $x$ (in liters) and the area \n $y$ (in square meters) she paints, as shown in the following table:\n\n| Liters of Paint $(x)$ | Square Meters $(y)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $0.12$ | $1$ |\n| $6$ | $50$ |\n| $12$ | $100$ |\n\n \n\nWhat is the equation for the relationship between $x$ and $y?$ Write the answer in terms of the variable $x.$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152522 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "352260eb-eee1-5b92-8634-81a951c9b6bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152522 #edb/ref 1074 "We're told that $x$ and $y$ have a proportional relationship.\n\nEvery proportional relationship can be written in the form $y=kx,$ where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.\n\nTo compute the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ from the table. Let's choose the point $(6,50),$ which corresponds to the second row of the table.\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{50}{6}=\\frac{25}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{25}{3}x.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152669 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152669 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68170" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152669 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152669 #edb/ref 1071 "Rick is driving on a highway. The relationship between the time $t$ (in minutes) he drives and the distance $d$ (in miles) he covers is shown in the table below.\n\n| Minutes ($t$) | Miles ($d$) |\n| --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $3$ |\n| $8$ | $6$ |\n| $12$ | $9$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. There is **not** a proportional relationship between $d$ and $t.$\n\nII. There is a proportional relationship between $d$ and $t.$\n\nIII. The constant of proportionality is $\\frac{3}{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152669 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a1a5194-704c-54ab-8cc7-ae51be039607" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152669 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's add a new column to our table and compute the ratio of miles per hour for each row. \n\n| Minutes ($t$) | Miles ($d$) | Miles / Minutes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $3$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| $8$ | $6$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| $12$ | $9$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n\n \n\nThe ratio of miles per minute is the same for each row. Therefore, there is a proportional relationship between $d$ and $t$  and the  constant of proportionality is $k=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153033 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153033 #edb/ref 1061 186936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153033 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8723b6ec-51b3-591f-8a78-8d66a882e2c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153033 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153034 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153034 #edb/ref 1061 186937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153034 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "86063632-706f-5896-a287-131371bbfac1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153034 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153035 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153035 #edb/ref 1061 186940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153035 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "174d042b-40fb-57f2-912b-20fe1548d308" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153035 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153036 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153036 #edb/ref 1061 186944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153036 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "387c9c18-edb6-507f-8ae3-d5a4d7a375b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153036 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153037 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153037 #edb/ref 1061 186945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153037 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4a43a0a6-a976-52ad-b7ac-89aca2144fe1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153037 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153038 #edb/ref 1061 186948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153038 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "faaa86eb-acf9-5373-90c9-af1a462a10aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153038 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153438 #edb/ref 1064 "Two variables, $x$ and $y,$ are said to have a **proportional relationship** if their ratio is constant. That is, for any $(x,y)$ pair, we have\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a fixed number called the **constant of proportionality** or **constant of variation.**\n\nAs a concrete example, let's consider the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ |\n| $4$ | $8$ |\n\nDo $x$ and $y$ have a proportional relationship?\n\nTo determine whether $x$ and $y$ have a proportional relationship, we first add a new $\\dfrac yx$ column:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $\\dfrac{y}{x}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |\n| $2$ | $4$ |  |\n| $4$ | $8$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\dfrac yx$ for each row:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $\\dfrac{y}{x}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $2\\div 1 = {\\color{blue}{2}}$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $4\\div 2 = {\\color{blue}{2}}$ |\n| $4$ | $8$ | $8\\div 4 = {\\color{blue}{2}}$ |\n\nSince all the entries in the third column have the same value, this table does indeed represent a proportional relationship. In this case, the constant of proportionality $k = {\\color{blue}{2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153438 #edb/ref 1065 8207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153438 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "935405f7-2fd3-5fb6-a9ef-e3d74aa721b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153438 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153439 #edb/ref 1064 "When the variables $x$ and $y$ form a proportional relationship, the ratio of any $(x,y)$ pair is constant:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac y x = k\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can rewrite this equation above as follows:\n\n$$\ny = kx\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153439 #edb/ref 1065 8208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153439 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d635aed4-07fa-5ffc-a25c-f9d760dbed7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153439 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Equation of a Proportional Relationship" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496688 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496688 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496688 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0f688d6b-922e-58ef-bcb3-12d4f73d4d03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496691 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496691 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496691 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "10287edb-ce0a-55d0-a65e-c99f015648c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496713 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496713 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 175921860454